FULL_STACK_DEVELOPER_2026_COMPLETE - Phase 04                                                          Health Endpoint, Configuration and Shutdown

Once HealthModule owns the working endpoint, the generated AppController and AppService no longer have a
responsibility and can be removed. This is cleanup based on responsibility, not cleanup for aesthetics.

Add a minimal startup configuration
main.ts - relevant section
async function bootstrap() {
const app = await NestFactory.create<NestFastifyApplication>(
AppModule,
new FastifyAdapter(),
);

app.enableShutdownHooks();

const port = Number(process.env.PORT ?? 4000);
await app.listen(port, '127.0.0.1');
}

At this phase, PORT is the only environment-aware value we need. We do not introduce @nestjs/config or a large
configuration system until configuration complexity exists.

Shutdown hooks

enableShutdownHooks() lets Nest participate in process shutdown signals. Later, database connections and background workers
will have meaningful cleanup work. Introducing the hook now establishes the lifecycle without creating those dependencies
early.

Verify without the frontend
Second PowerShell window
Invoke-RestMethod -Uri 'http://127.0.0.1:4000/health' -Method Get

Expected PowerShell object view
status service
------ -------
ok       taskflow-api

Checkpoint D

GET /health succeeds independently. The backend is now a verifiable application even though TaskFlow web has not called it
yet.

Official source notes
This phase is written against NestJS 12.0.3 and the Nest CLI 12.0.3 release line. Fastify remains on the stable v5 line; the September 17, 2026
stable patch reference is 5.12.5. The course does not adopt Fastify 6 alpha builds.

Primary references: docs.nestjs.com/modules, docs.nestjs.com/providers, docs.nestjs.com/techniques/performance,
github.com/nestjs/nest/releases, github.com/nestjs/nest-cli/releases, github.com/fastify/fastify/releases.

